inline_comments.comment:
This `while` loop creates an infinite async retry storm when `setupWatchers` completes but establishes no watchers.

Scenario: the project is not inside a git repo, so `getRepoRoot()` (called inside `getGitDir()`) throws. The `setupWatchers` catch block swallows the error, leaving `this.watchers` empty. The `finally` block then resets `watcherSetupInFlight` to `undefined`. On the next loop iteration both conditions are still true, so a new `setupWatchers` call is launched — repeating indefinitely. Because `getRepoRoot()` doesn't cache a failed result, every iteration spawns a fresh `execa` call to `git rev-parse`.

A simple fix is to drop the `while` loop and only attempt setup once per `ensureWatchers` invocation:

```ts
private async ensureWatchers(): Promise<void> {
  if (Object.keys(this.watchers).length > 0) return;
  if (!this.watcherSetupInFlight && this.gitStateCallbacks.size > 0) {
    this.watcherSetupInFlight = this.setupWatchers(this.watcherSetupGeneration);
  }
  await this.watcherSetupInFlight;
}
```

Concurrent `onGitStateChange` callers are still handled correctly (they await the same in-flight promise), but there is no retry when setup fails to produce any watchers.

Lines 1 and 3 both import from `'node:fs'` and should be merged into a single import statement:

```ts
import type { FSWatcher, watch } from 'node:fs';
import type { readFile, stat } from 'node:fs/promises';
```

Splitting imports from the same module across multiple declarations adds noise and can trip import-order linters.

`void callback` is an unusual idiom for suppressing an unused-parameter warning. The conventional approach in TypeScript is to prefix the parameter name with `_`:

```ts
readonly onGitStateChangeMock = vi.fn<(callback: () => void) => () => void>((_callback) => {
  return vi.fn();
});
```

This is self-documenting, avoids the `void` expression whose only purpose is a side-effect-free evaluation, and works consistently with linter rules.

general_comments.comment:
There is no test for `GitDiffProvider.onGitStateChange` when the project is not inside a git repository (i.e. when `getRepoRoot()` / `execa` throws). This is the exact scenario that triggers the infinite retry loop described in the `ensureWatchers` inline comment. A test along these lines would both document the expected behaviour and serve as a regression guard:

```ts
it('does not retry indefinitely when git is unavailable', async () => {
  // Arrange: mock execa to reject so getRepoRoot() fails
  vi.mocked(execa).mockRejectedValue(new Error('not a git repository'));
  const provider = new GitDiffProvider('/not-a-repo');
  const callback = vi.fn();

  const unsubscribe = provider.onGitStateChange(callback);
  await Promise.resolve(); // flush microtasks
  await Promise.resolve();

  // Assert: watcher setup ran exactly once and did not spin
  expect(vi.mocked(execa)).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  expect(callback).not.toHaveBeenCalled();

  unsubscribe();
});
```

In `configureBranchWatcher`, `this.clearWatcher('branch')` is called explicitly at the start of the method, and then `watchFile` unconditionally calls `this.clearWatcher(key)` again internally before registering the new watcher. The redundant pre-clear is intentional — it closes the stale watcher promptly before the async `readHeadRef` call, preventing a window where the old watcher could fire — but this intent is not obvious to readers. A short inline comment would prevent future maintainers from treating it as dead code and removing it:

```ts
// Close the stale branch watcher immediately (before the async readHeadRef)
// so it can't fire spuriously while we're reconfiguring.
this.clearWatcher('branch');
```
